 I felt so  guilty that I'd be able to get on a  plane and leave a lot of these places  and the suffering state yeah the people  were stuck in that in their reality long  after the headlines were gone but I  think you learned to Co  I am realizing it is mission it's not  it's not just a job after leave of  absence Guzzi returned to the post and  again began traveling the world covering  humanitarian conflicts from India to  Ethiopia making France along the way a  photo story on child amputees from  Sierra Leone resulted in a number of  adoptions including a young girl named  mimouna who became goosies goddaughter  but again and again she returned to  Haiti  the country was ravaged by an earthquake  in 2010 it shook Guzzi to her core I  love the people and I love the spirit  and it was like my second home almost so  and when the earthquake happened that  just about destroyed me her coverage of  the recovery effort earned Guzzi and her  colleagues an historic fourth Pulitzer  goosies pictures now hang alongside  hundreds of other Pulitzer prize-winning  photographs at the Newseum a testament  to the power of images to change history  like kevin carter's 1993 picture of a  starving sudanese girl  William Beals 1957 picture of a  policeman talking with a young boy this  is only you know the tip of the iceberg  when you think of all the images that  have been made goosies photographs often  show hope even in the darkest of  circumstances it's what she searches for  when she arrives in a place like Haiti  like this this poor little girl and  three hundred thousand other people they  they were gone in a whisper or worse  buried under you know layers of concrete  and died days later weeks later even  when you see that how do you just keep  moving to take those photographs I tend  to always look for those moments of hope  too because I think we need balance we  need to show people not only the  problems but sometimes the solution too  and it saves my faith in humanity for  s